= Sequence annotation =

== Overview ==
'''Sequence annotation''' is the process of attaching structured features, labels, evidence, or interpretations to biological sequences, such as genes, coding regions, domains, regulatory elements, variants, or predicted functions.

Annotation can be produced experimentally, manually curated from literature, transferred from related sequences, or predicted computationally. These evidence routes have different uncertainty and should not be silently conflated.

Genome annotation often includes structural annotation—locating genes and other features—and functional annotation—assigning putative roles or relationships. Protein annotation can include domains, active sites, localization, interactions, and function.

Annotations change as evidence, assemblies, algorithms, and reference databases improve. Version and provenance are therefore part of the scientific meaning of an annotation.

== Boundaries ==
* annotation ≠ sequence
* predicted annotation ≠ experimentally validated function
* annotation version ≠ timeless fact

